TPC-H-shaped retail CPU configurations
======================================

Every retail source exposes default, par, radix, radix_retained, no-reduce,
transfer, le, le_runs, and advised. Kernel and full-query shells also retain
the matched built_hash/built_radix materialization comparison.
Standalone/staged runners use standalone_configs! and compile the entire authored
runner for each configuration; the same CQ is not copied between configurations.
Only advised applies stage-local query! advice. Transfer applies
cq_planners::order(transfer) independently to each authored CQ stage, retaining
the same preprocessing, materialization, aggregate heads, and answer contracts.
Most advice follows experiments/gpu/tpch/src/advice.rs; H05 retains its CPU
cycle-closing order and H09 its connected Part-first written order.
See tpch-methodology.txt for pipeline, timing, and correctness semantics.

The provider comparisons retain load-time StringCode interning in every coded
kernel, including the explicitly authored runners. Dictionary literals and
load-time predicate memberships remain coded during execution; text is published
only at the existing DECODE answer sites. H09 keeps its GreenNames membership
before the connected Lineitem/PartSupp prefix in both written and advised order.

All 29 entries in src/tpch.rs CASES expose default and transfer: 27 retail-derived
cases plus full tpch_q06 and tpch_q19, which retain their own configuration shells.
This inventory is not the complete canonical ordered TPC-H Q1-Q22 suite.
No input projection or cached data schema changes are needed for transfer.
The historical validation table below does not report transfer validation.
Current transfer validation covers all 29 cases at SF0.1 and SF1, with exact
DuckDB witnesses and separate deterministic counters. See
experiments/gpu/compare/rpt-repair/summary.json from the repository root for the full
matrix, H09 timeout control, paired timing method and remaining limitations.

Contract-7 CPU representation regression
----------------------------------------
Comparing HEAD 4944b917 with the explicit-where migration at VO, relational-plan,
Physical, Structures and Sequence showed preserved range reads and join plans.
The eight SF1 regressions first diverged in Structures: H04 and D27 lost dense
membership-only builds, while H03/H05/H07/H08/D19/D85 replaced dense join filters
with NoDense hash-and-sorted-key providers. H04/D27 also acquired redundant
supplementary filters over their membership-only builds. H07 gained two scalar
nation filters; its existing three large filters lost dense storage.

AdaptiveMembership now records the same type-blind provider and bitmap budget
for every eligible key. Rust capability dispatch, not compiler inspection of
column types or Rust guards, selects a total injective DenseOrdinal codec.
Arbitrary Eq + Hash keys and oversized spans retain exact hash membership;
contains-only fallback does not need Ord or a sorted interval index.
Membership-only builds do not also construct the redundant scan filter.
Automatic supplementary scan-filter selection retains its separate opt-in
policy: only recorded payload-index families (HashIndex, SortedRun, and their
routed forms) supply them, not HashSet merely because it can enumerate keys.
This is a selection cost policy, not a validity restriction: explicitly selected
custom single-level providers remain legal when they satisfy the covering
probe and `Keys` contracts. Rust autoref dispatch
enables the scalar policy for i64 (including aliases); other scalar types skip
key counting, enumeration, and construction before the selectivity check.
Identity-only filters do not make a previously unknown source population known.
The runtime exposes an explicit identity fact for absent filters. Scalar and
chunked block reads enumerate positions directly for identity predicates,
without extracting keys, reading bounds, or calling row predicates; combined
reads omit only the identity operand. Generated supplementary position tests
also check the identity fact before extracting a key. Instrumentation remains
attached to actual tested rows rather than hiding redundant predicate work.
These gates preserve the earlier filter workload without inspecting column-type
spellings in the compiler; membership codec capability alone is not an opt-in.
The representation law and conformance row are STR-AdaptiveMembership in
doc/formal/ir_structures.tex and doc/formal/conformance/front.md.

Individual SF1 default measurements (2026-10-28, median of three, milliseconds):

| Query | CPU reference | Regressed | Repaired | Repaired/reference |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| retail_h04 | 50.404 | 628.212 | 50.848 | 1.009 |
| retail_d27 | 18.064 | 60.936 | 18.408 | 1.019 |
| retail_h07 | 39.322 | 75.577 | 36.744 | 0.934 |
| retail_h08 | 39.276 | 71.852 | 36.797 | 0.937 |
| retail_d19 | 21.450 | 34.618 | 21.573 | 1.006 |
| retail_h05 | 41.987 | 64.069 | 42.084 | 1.002 |
| retail_h03 | 57.806 | 82.220 | 42.845 | 0.741 |
| retail_d85 | 28.429 | 39.656 | 28.382 | 0.998 |

All eight matched the independent DuckDB oracle exactly. Commands were
  cargo xtask bench --tpch --sf 1 --runs 3 --query QUERY --config default
from features/workloads. CPU references are from
experiments/gpu/compare/performance_cpu_sf1.tsv at repository root.
Reports are target/bench/tpch-1-QUERY-config-default.tsv. Boundary captures,
numbered IR, diffs and the preserved regressed counters are in repository-root
target/tpch-regression-ir. H04 restored the reference's 109863 hash operations,
57218 lookups and one index build (regressed: 4752814, 109741 and two).
D27 restored 1510000 rows tested, avoiding the redundant filter's additional
6001215 lineitem row tests. Other large filters restored the reference hash
counts; H07's two additional tiny dense filters further reduce hash work.

The final full default sweep (`--tpch --sf 1 --runs 3 --config default`) passed
all 29 exact comparisons. Every query with a CPU reference was below 1.15x;
the largest ratio was retail_h22 at 1.099x. retail_h09, tpch_q06 and tpch_q19
have no default reference row in performance_cpu_sf1.tsv, so no ratio is claimed
for them. The full report is target/bench/tpch-1-config-default.tsv; the joined
reference/regressed/repaired table is repository-root
target/tpch-regression-ir/summary.tsv. No contract-7 semantic exception was used.
Root `cargo test --workspace` passed 1095 tests across 138 suites after registering
the adaptive provider's native Sequence membership capability. Runtime consumer
tests cover dense integer/borrowed-singleton keys, arbitrary non-Ord exact-hash
keys, sparse fallback, custom capabilities, and unchanged custom Build providers.

SF0.1 configuration validation (2026-10-24)
------------------------------------------
Each cell below was exercised with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 and:

  cargo xtask bench --tpch --sf 0.1 --runs 1 --query retail_QUERY --config CONFIG

Run from features/workloads. "exact" means the selected configuration's TSV
correctness column is "pass": exact complete-answer comparison with DuckDB,
including warm, measured, and independently instrumented counter executions.
Raw reports are target/bench/tpch-0.1-retail_QUERY-config-CONFIG.tsv and .json;
command logs are target/bench/config-validation/QUERY-CONFIG.log.
These one-run checks validate answers, not stable performance rankings.

The 26-query GPU comparison suite excludes H14. H14 is additionally checked
here because it remains a standalone CPU catalog query and uses the same macro.
No LE or le_runs configuration was rejected by the planner.

